WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.740
When you get down to it, the games we play and enjoy all have an element of strategy.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.640
Whether that's a serious one…

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:23.560
(Casting)

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.400
...or not.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:45.920
(Casting)

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:54.320
But while picking your approach is part of the game, some tactics are seen as too controversial, too... cheesy.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:18.440
(Casting)

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:24.440
And like its delicious real-life counterpart – gaming cheese is varied, versatile and

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.280
very much a product of the culture from which it derives.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.240
"Hey man you want some cheese before we roll out?"

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.200
Besides the obvious “cheese-like” meaning, it looks like the term cheesy began to mean

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.840
“cheap” or “inferior” around the turn of the 20th century in the United States.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.760
But in gaming, the earliest existing examples actually refer to Street Fighter II.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.450
And before we get too far into this, keep in mind that the term has since been used

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.760
loosely and differently in a variety of games.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.670
We’re going to look at a spread of fine gaming cheeses.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:00.140
In Street Fighter, the term called attention to strategies that were seen as disproportionately

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:06.020
powerful compared to the skill required to use them, like spamming the same move repeatedly.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:11.480
Relying on a cheap strategy like that was, in most gaming communities, seen as dishonorable.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:18.720
And in 1994’s bizarre post-apocalyptic fighting game Primal Rage, Atari Games added a “No

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.480
Cheese” feature: which may be the first time that dishonorable play was specifically

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.140
called Cheese in-game and not just by its community.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:31.600
"No cheese?"

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:32.700
"No cheese?"

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.720
"I don't know, I think it was cause I was doing the same thing over and over."

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.860
The “no cheese” mechanic was triggered when a player used the same move too many

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:47.440
times in a combo or tried to throw an opponent who was still in blockstun – resulting in

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.760
the “No Cheese” notice appearing on-screen.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.880
When “no cheese” is active, the offender’s repeated move no longer damages their opponent

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:59.360
and, if it's used during a chained attack, causes the combo to be dropped.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:04.990
By the way, if you can’t tell yet, this game is f*cking crazy and extremely edgy

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:05.720
for the 90s.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.320
Just look at this sh*t!

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.560
“Now that's f*cked up” Anyways, Stephen Riesenberger was an associate producer on

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.520
Primal Rage, and he told me in an email that No Cheese was meant to prevent infinite combos

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.720
— but also that the idea acted as a sort of substitute for just making cheese strategies

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:40.740
ineffective.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:47.120
And this is the first important part of cheese: as games became more well-balanced, a cheesy

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:53.340
tactic increasingly referred to a shallow strategy, something that wasn’t really consistently

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.940
effective against experienced players.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:04:01.380
Following Primal Rage, Capcom put a cheese icon in Street Fighter Alpha II, that appeared

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:07.060
when you won a round by defeating a blocking opponent with chip damage from a super or special move.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:13.040
(Casting)

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.820
While the concept of cheese in gaming may have been well-known in ‘90s fighting games,

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:23.980
the term exploded in popularity with StarCraft and StarCraft 2 – the latter specifically

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.000
popularizing it in the West.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.260
(Casting)

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:33.220
"So it looks like we are gonna have some sort of cheese on our hands."

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.700
Much like in fighting games,

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:39.300
cheese strategies were frowned upon in Starcraft, but what made them different in Blizzard’s

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:44.860
RTS is they relied on a lack of information — either not knowing that was happening,

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.720
or not knowing how to counter it.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:56.400
(Casting)

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:04.320
StarCraft cheese is usually an all-in proposition – you either live

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.920
by it or you die by it, and its success is entirely dependent on how prepared for it

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.200
your opponent is.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.680
"Now this guy has balls of steel Artosis."

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.380
"In map number one he tries something incredibly risky  because if it's scouted and spotted,

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.060
it is very easy to handle."

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.240
"And you ask, "Do you think he's gonna do it again?"

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.100
"I said no, I think a lot of other people would say no."

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:29.260
"But he's doing the same again."

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:38.180
(Casting)

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.540
"We've been around Starcraft competition for years,

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:45.220
people have loved these moves, Koreans particular they'll either end a series or start it with some

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:49.940
pretty ballsy cheese, just to throw off their opponent. But to do it twice,

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.100
is that insanity, or is that bravery."

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:58.540
And with the MOBA trend in full swing toward the end of the 2000s, cheese began to develop

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.960
more unique flavours, from suspicious looking team comp fromage:

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:19.520
(Casting)

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.800
...to unpredictable, game-changing strats au gratin:

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:44.680
(Casting)

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:50.960
Following Dota allstars, Valve even went as far as making cheese an in-game item in Dota 2.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:55.760
Cheese is a powerful consumable dropped by Roshan after his second death and every time

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:56.640
thereafter.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.400
Roshan's cheese restores a sizable portion of its indulger’s health and mana when used,

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.040
making it pretty gouda.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.020
"Relax it was just a pun."

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:10.600
"SHUT THE F*CK UP. Okay? SHUT THE F*CK UP.”

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:15.920
Today, the MOBA genre has become massive, and it’s predominantly here and in StarCraft

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.400
that the term “cheese” remains in use.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.610
But some argue that “cheese” in MOBAs, especially at the competitive level,

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:26.520
has become an unnecessarily derogatory term for what would otherwise be called a “pocket

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.919
strat”: something innovative that catches the opponent off-guard.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.040
Essentially, one player’s pocket strat could be another player’s cheese.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.680
"It ain't easy! Being cheesy!"

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:40.820
But as long as we play games, there will be those who will gravitate towards cheap,

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:42.300
easy-to-use strategies.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.140
And while not all of us may like cheese, it's become an accepted part of every competitive

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:47.640
game.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:48.600
"Chees!"

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.580
But you know what's not acceptable?

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:56.120
I specifically asked for an assorted cheese platter, and you gave me this!

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:03.400
These are like the Protoss Cannon Rush of dairy products.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:08.480
Did you see that? One of their fatalities is he just makes their ghost go into heaven.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:09.540
I don't know why.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.380
And then one of Chaos' finishers I don't know if you saw it, he just literally jumps into a lake.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.120
I mean we can swim like apes probably.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:21.660
Yeah. Butterfly.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:25.960
Oh yeah.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:30.960
Shut up I wouldn't drown!

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:33.000
I can swim!

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.380
I don't know what it's called but I can swim dude I'm not gonna drown.

